For a better understanding of the SP-MC conductance changes, details of the charge transfer were calculated. The charge transfer in organic materials can be best described by charge hopping using Marcus theory.[30] The rate of the charge hopping between two weakly coupled sites is proportional to the square of the electronic coupling elements, J. To calculate the electronic coupling between SP, MC and their mixture (1:1), we used the Quantum Patch approach[31] for the pairs of photoswitchable molecules placed in adjacent unit cells of the MOF as described in SI. The electronic coupling, which strongly depends on the hopping distance, Figure 4b, is in the range of $0.001-10\mathsf{m e V}$ for all studied systems, providing charge transport in the photochromic MOF film. The coupling decreases with increasing distance. At a distance between the hopping sites of $1.37\:\mathsf{n m}$ , i.e. corresponding to the experimentally estimated average value, the MC-MC coupling is still relatively high, i.e. $1-10\mathsf{m e V}$ .
The electronic coupling between the MC molecules is generally larger than the coupling between the SP molecules, for the HOMO as well as for the LUMO orbitals, Figure 4b. While at small distances, the MC coupling for the HOMO orbitals is approximately 4 times larger than the SP coupling, it is roughly two orders of magnitude larger at longer molecular distances, in particular at the experimentally estimated average value. This larger electron coupling of MC in comparison to SP results in an increased charge hopping rates. Assuming that the reorganization energies and the Gibbs free energies are not affected by the SP-MC isomerization, the larger MC coupling results in higher electronic conductance, proportional to the square of the coupling energy.[30] As a result, while for small molecular distance the conductance of MC is approximately 16 times higher than that of SP, the MC conductance may be up to 4 orders of magnitude larger than the SP conductance at large molecular distances. In the experiment, $70\%$ of the photoswitches are in the MC state, thus, most charge hops occur between MC-MC and SP-MC, explaining the measured phenomenon.
Since the HOMO and LUMO of MC show similarly efficient charge transfer, we investigate the ionization potential (IP) and electron affinity (EA) to evaluate whether electron or hole conduction is prevailing, see Table SI1. The DFT calculations show that due to the more efficient hole injection from the gold electrode (work function of approx. 5.1 eV), the electron hole transport substantially dominates, especially for MC whose IP value is significantly (by $0.48\:\Theta\backslash I$ closer to the gold electrode than the IP of SP. It should be noted that in addition to the higher coupling between the molecules, further effects might affect the electronic conductivity of the SP@UiO-67 films, like the fact that MC is a zwitterionic molecule possessing charges with higher mobility. Based on the results for spiropyran monolayers[17], where conductance changes of up to a factor of 35 were observed (with tunneling rather than hopping as fundamental charge transfer mechanism) as well as based on the DFT calculations, where an even larger on-off-ratio were predicted, we believe the photoswitching effect can be further increased by optimizing the distance and the orientation of the embedded photoswitches. This could be done by optimizing the host MOF structure as well as by optimizing the structure and the loading of the photoswitches.
In conclusion, we presented a photochromic MOF material of light-responsive spiropyran embedded in UiO-67-MOF-films. Upon UV-irradiation, spiropyran isomerizes to merocyanine which goes along with changes in microscopic parameters like molecular length, ionization potential and extension of the conjugated $\scriptstyle{\frac{}{}}\pi.$ -electron system, resulting in a reversible increase of the electronic conductance of the spiropyran@UiO-67-MOF-film by one order of magnitude. Detailed DFT calculations unveil that the higher conductance of the merocyanine form is a result of the larger electronic coupling between the molecules and the hole injection from the gold electrode, resulting in efficient electron hole conduction.
Although the simple embedment of photochromic molecules is a straightforward way to functionalize the MOF material,[5d] the controlled immobilization of the photoswitches at defined positions in the periodic framework remains a future task. This will enable precise calculations of the conduction processes in regular systems[32] and switching of the directed, anisotropic conductance.
